    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Every Corpus Christi hurricane season brings the same question for property owners: if a storm hits, will my insurance actually work the way I expect? On the Coastal Bend, the answer often depends on three different policies, a percentage deductible, and how well you documented the property before and after the storm. This Corpus Christi hurricane season guide covers the dates, the history, and simple checklists for before, during, and after a storm, with a focus on what helps your insurance claim. For evacuation orders and life-safety decisions, always follow local officials and the <a href="https://www.weather.gov/crp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service in Corpus Christi</a>.</p>

<h2>Hurricane season dates and peak</h2>
<p>The Atlantic hurricane season runs from <strong>June 1 to November 30</strong>. The <a href="https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/climo/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Hurricane Center's climatology</a> places the peak around September 10, with most activity from mid-August to mid-October. Coastal Bend history shows that early-season storms matter too: Hurricane Hanna made landfall on Padre Island on July 25, 2020, and NWS Corpus Christi lists Hurricane Claudette (July 15, 2003) among the area's major events.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/weather/">Corpus Christi weather page</a> shows live National Weather Service conditions, forecasts, and alerts, active storms from the National Hurricane Center, and NOAA tide data for Corpus Christi Bay, Packery Channel, and Port Aransas.</p>

<h2>Coastal Bend hurricane history</h2>
<p>A few storms shape how local insurers, adjusters, and building officials think about risk:</p>
