<h2>Know your three policies</h2>
<p>Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties are all in the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) designated catastrophe area. Many coastal owners therefore carry:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A homeowners or commercial policy</strong> that may exclude wind and hail and covers other perils such as fire and many plumbing leaks.</li>
<li><strong>A TWIA windstorm policy</strong> that covers wind and hail only. TWIA is not flood insurance.</li>
<li><strong>A flood policy</strong> through the NFIP or a private insurer. Standard homeowners policies generally exclude flood, and NFIP residential limits are $250,000 for the building and $100,000 for contents.</li>
</ul>
<p>After a hurricane, the most common dispute is wind versus water: did wind tear off the roof and let rain in, or did surge and rising water cause the damage? Each policy pays only for its own peril, so the way the damage is documented matters. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge damage claims</a> explains how that split is usually analyzed.</p>

<h2>Before the storm: insurance and property checklist</h2>
<p>The best time to prepare a claim is before there is one. Work through this list early in the season:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pull your declarations pages</strong> for every policy. Note the dwelling limit (Coverage A), other structures, contents, loss of use, and any ordinance or law coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Find your deductibles.</strong> Many Texas policies carry a separate wind/hail or named-storm deductible, often a percentage of Coverage A. Estimate yours with our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> and read about <a href="/named-storm-hurricane-deductibles-texas/">named-storm and hurricane deductibles in Texas</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Buy or confirm flood coverage now.</strong> NFIP coverage generally starts 30 days after purchase, so waiting until a storm forms is usually too late. Properties in V, VE, or V1-30 zones built or altered on or after September 1, 2009 must show proof of NFIP coverage, when available, to get TWIA coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Locate your windstorm certificate.</strong> For TWIA eligibility, work done on or after January 1, 1988 needs a certificate of compliance (WPI-8). Keep a copy with your policy documents. See our article on <a href="/windstorm-certificate-wpi-8-insurance-claims/">WPI-8 windstorm certificates</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Document the property.</strong> Take date-stamped photos and video of every exterior elevation, the roof if you can do so safely, every room, and major contents. Save receipts for big purchases and recent upgrades such as a new roof or HVAC.</li>
<li><strong>Store copies off-site.</strong> Upload policies, photos, and receipts to cloud storage or email them to yourself.</li>
<li><strong>Check the property.</strong> Clear gutters and drains, trim trees near the house, secure loose items, and plan how you will protect windows and doors.</li>
<li><strong>Write down claim contacts.</strong> Keep policy numbers and claim phone numbers for your homeowners carrier, TWIA, and flood insurer where you can reach them without power.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our printable <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through the same steps.</p>

