<h2>During the storm</h2>
<ul>
<li>Follow evacuation orders and official guidance. No property or claim is worth risking your safety.</li>
<li>Monitor NWS alerts, NHC advisories, and tide levels on our <a href="/weather/">live weather tools page</a>.</li>
<li>If you are sheltering in place and it is safe, note the times you hear or see major damage, such as roof failure, broken windows, or water entering. Timing can help separate wind damage from later surge or flooding.</li>
<li>Do not go outside during the eye of a storm or to inspect damage while winds are still high.</li>
</ul>

<h2>After the storm: documenting TWIA, flood, and homeowners claims</h2>
<p>Once it is safe, the first days matter most for evidence.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Photograph before you clean up.</strong> Capture wide shots of each side of the building and close-ups of damage. Photograph water lines on walls, debris lines outside, and damaged items before they are thrown away. Video walk-throughs help.</li>
<li><strong>Record the water.</strong> Measure and photograph the height of any flood or surge line inside and outside. This supports the flood claim and helps show which damage was above the water line and likely wind-related.</li>
<li><strong>Make temporary repairs.</strong> Tarp roofs, board openings, and remove standing water to prevent further damage, and keep every receipt. Most policies require reasonable steps to protect the property.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim to each insurer.</strong> Report wind and hail damage to TWIA or your wind carrier, flood damage to your flood insurer, and other damage to your homeowners or commercial carrier. Write down claim numbers and the names of everyone you speak with.</li>
<li><strong>Keep damaged items when you can.</strong> Insurers may want to see them. If health or safety requires disposal, photograph them first and keep samples of materials such as flooring or roofing.</li>
<li><strong>Track additional living expenses.</strong> If the home is unlivable, keep hotel, meal, and mileage receipts for loss of use coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Be careful with contractors.</strong> Read any agreement before signing, and do not sign documents that assign your insurance benefits without understanding them.</li>
</ol>
<p>For more detail, see our <a href="/hurricane-damage-claims-corpus-christi-guide/">guide to Corpus Christi hurricane damage claims</a>, our <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> page, and our article on <a href="/noaa-weather-records-document-property-damage-corpus-christi/">using NOAA weather records to document property damage</a>.</p>

<h2>Common mistakes after a hurricane</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reporting only to one insurer.</strong> If you have homeowners, TWIA, and flood policies, report to each one that may apply. A denial from one does not mean the others will not pay.</li>
<li><strong>Throwing out evidence too early.</strong> Wet drywall, flooring, and contents often must be removed quickly to prevent mold, but photograph and measure them first.</li>
<li><strong>Accepting the first estimate without review.</strong> Early estimates after a catastrophe can miss items such as code-required upgrades, interior water damage that appears later, or damaged elevations that were not inspected. Compare the estimate line by line with the damage you documented, and see our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Missing hidden damage.</strong> Wind-driven rain can soak insulation and wall cavities without obvious stains at first. Watch for musty odors, staining, or bubbling paint in the weeks after a storm.</li>
<li><strong>Forgetting recoverable depreciation.</strong> Under replacement cost policies, insurers typically pay actual cash value first and release recoverable depreciation after repairs, within a deadline set by the policy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Claim deadlines to keep in mind</h2>
<p>Deadlines depend on the insurer and policy. For TWIA, Texas Insurance Code Chapter 2210 sets these timelines:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>TWIA step</th><th>Timeline</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>File the claim</td><td>No later than one year after the date of damage (extension of up to 180 days possible for good cause)</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA requests missing information</td><td>Within 30 days after the claim is filed</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA accepts, partly accepts, or denies</td><td>Within 60 days after receiving the claim or requested information (the Commissioner may extend)</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA pays an accepted claim</td><td>Within 10 days after the notice of acceptance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Demand appraisal on the amount</td><td>Within 60 days after receiving the acceptance or partial acceptance notice</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Private insurers follow the Texas Prompt Payment of Claims Act in Chapter 542, and after a weather-related catastrophe declared by the Commissioner those deadlines are extended by 15 days. Use our <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> to track dates. Deadlines to sue are legal questions; consult a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>When to get help</h2>
